1. **Gate 1:** the proposed two-list code check cannot catch a part omitted from both lists, so I agree to close that contract. The other listed results also stand. But they do not prove that every possible new version has failed, or that changing a model necessarily changes credentials or provider caps. Those constraints must be checked for a specific model and transport; no switch is currently justified by the record.

   The reference comparison is a lead, not a model ranking: the Claude writer had **0 agreed faults on 26**, the Codex writer **8**, and the frozen step **3** in one stored draw. The prewritten reference-control criterion was not met. The controls worked on those examples; they do not establish that the readers are universally valid. [Reference result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/reference-control/RESULT.md)

2. **The decision page should report the impasse, not assign two choices exclusively to Alex.** State that gate 1 is unmet; the tested repairs did not yield an accepted candidate; a different model is an unproven lead; Alex’s six lines and D426 remain unchanged; and the running system remains the default. Reopen the gate-1 investigation on a **specific, authorized candidate with a decision-changing test**, or on Alex’s overriding direction. D439 gives the council reversible choices while he is inactive; it does not permit changing the frozen ruler or credentials and provider caps. [Current state](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:220)

3. **One next step: a $0 feasibility design for a paired gate-3 comparison on the same matters.** Determine whether both systems’ stored libraries and remaining news can support comparisons of genuinely equivalent questions, while separately counting important matters either system misses. Size the test against a decision-relevant difference, with uncertainty about repeated draws, reader disagreement, library reconstruction and total cost explicit. The existing side-by-side shows why equivalence must be checked: on China approvals, one system asks about U.S. permission and the other combines U.S. and Chinese permission. [Side-by-side](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/side-by-side/SIDE-BY-SIDE-WEEK.md)

   **Moves the decision:** a reviewed design can detect a specified meaningful difference at a defensible cost within the remaining ceiling, with credible controls and enough genuinely comparable matters. The council can then decide whether to send it and, separately, settle the numerical meaning of “beats.” **Closes it:** the available material, comparability or sensitivity cannot support such a decision within the ceiling. This does not pass gate 3 or change the frozen ruler.

   This is a targeted check of whether pairing changes the earlier sizing conclusion, not another unpriced full comparison. The earlier estimate put an affordable unpaired comparison’s detection limit around **31 percentage points or more** under optimistic assumptions, with reliability uncertain. [Comparison sizing](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/comparison/COMPARISON-SIZING.md)

**CONSENSUS: no, because the record supports “no accepted gate-1 candidate,” not exhaustion or deferral; next, assess the paired gate-3 comparison at $0 under D439.**
